 \section{An audit list for comparisons of distance measures}
 \label{sec:protocol}
 
 \begin{framed}
 \noindent\textbf{Before reporting that a distance measure changes a result}
 \begin{enumerate}
-\item \textbf{Audit the origin.} Recode the opinion space by an affine shift and rerun a few seeds; any outcome that changes depends on the origin. Centre the space at a meaningful neutral point, or say why not, and treat every shrinkage or decay target (trust, attention, forgetting) as an explicit, varied assumption.
-\item \textbf{Do not rely on a shared threshold value.} A shared $\varepsilon$ admits a different share of pairs under each metric. If one is used, show that realised coverage matches.
-\item \textbf{Report the response to $\varepsilon$, not one matched point.} Plot outcomes against \emph{realised} coverage across a range of thresholds. A point matched at the start and a point matched over the run answer different questions (the effect including, or excluding, the metric's effect on how often agents interact); name the one you report.
-\item \textbf{Check for monotone equivalence.} Metrics related by a strictly increasing function are one gate once matched (Lemma~\ref{lem:monotone}); differences between them come from the update rule or the measurement.
-\item \textbf{Test the comparison.} A claim that a metric changes an effect is an interaction; test it directly \citep{gelman2006}, and support ``no difference'' with an equivalence test \citep{lakens2017}, not a non-significant one.
-\item \textbf{Report the ruler and the stopping time.} Count groups with one rule for every metric, report the share of runs that have not settled, and show that conclusions survive a longer run.
+\item \textbf{Audit the origin} (new). Recode the opinion space by an affine shift and rerun a few seeds; any outcome that changes depends on the origin. Check every operation in the box of Section~\ref{sec:background}, centre the space at a meaningful neutral point or say why not, and treat every shrinkage or decay target as an explicit, varied assumption.
+\item \textbf{Do not rely on a shared threshold value} (extends \citealp{lorenz2008}). A shared $\varepsilon$ admits a different share of pairs under each distance measure. If one is used, show that the pass rates match.
+\item \textbf{Report the response to $\varepsilon$, not one matched point} (new). Plot outcomes against the pass rate across a range of thresholds. Start-, early- and run-matched points answer different questions (the effect including, or excluding, a geometry's effect on how often agents interact); name the one you report.
+\item \textbf{Check for monotone equivalence}. Distance measures related by a strictly increasing function are one gate once matched (Lemma~\ref{lem:monotone}); differences between them come from the update step or the measurement.
+\item \textbf{Test the comparison} (standard: \citealp{gelman2006,lakens2017}). A claim that a geometry changes an effect is an interaction; test it directly, and support ``no difference'' with an equivalence test, not a non-significant one.
+\item \textbf{Report the ruler and the stopping time} (the ruler is new). Count groups with one rule for every geometry, report the share of runs that have not settled, and show that conclusions survive a longer run.
 \end{enumerate}
 \end{framed}
+\noindent Applying the list costs a fraction of the comparison itself: the origin audit and the ruler need a few reruns or a recount of saved states, the response curves about ten thresholds per geometry, and run-matching one bisection per cell, which took \VCalIterMean\ steps on average and cost about \VCalCostShare\ of the main runs' compute in our minimal model.
 
 \section{Limitations}
-Opinions start uniform and independent; clustered or correlated starting opinions change how coverage depends on the metric, though not the argument for reporting it.
-We study pairwise Deffuant updates only; for Hegselmann--Krause averaging the natural analogue of coverage is the mean number of neighbours inside the confidence region, which we did not test.
+Opinions start uniform and independent; clustered or correlated starting opinions change how coverage depends on the geometry, though not the argument for reporting it.
+We study pairwise Deffuant updates only; for Hegselmann--Krause averaging the natural analogue of coverage is the mean number of neighbours inside the confidence region, which we did not test, and we did not vary the convergence rate $\mu$, the population size, the network or heterogeneous and asymmetric thresholds.
+Runs stop at 4{,}000 rounds, when \VUnsettledFourAll\ of runs have not settled, so effects that appear only later would be missed.
 The case study rests on one model; its trust mechanism is specific to it, while the general point (multiplicative steps inherit the meaning of the origin) is not.
 Our list of hidden choices is not exhaustive: the network, the update order and the size of the population are choices of the same kind.
 
