+\paragraph{Purpose.} To study how a population's views on AI regulation respond to a policy, to media and to shocks; here, how a schedule of shocks changes the final spread of opinions at different enforcement lags.
+
+\paragraph{Entities, state variables and scales.} 120 agents on a Barab\'asi--Albert network ($m=3$). Each agent has a four-dimensional opinion in $[-1,1]^4$ (deployment cap, transparency, enforcement, audit intensity; 0 is neutral), an ideology in $[-1,1]$, a trust $\tau_i \in [0,1]$ and an influence weight that grows with its degree. The policy is a fixed point in the same space, read from a policy text. Runs last 60 ticks.
+
+\paragraph{Process overview and scheduling.} Each tick: (1) at tick 30 the policy tightens (enforcement $+0.3$, audit intensity $+0.2$); (2) shocks, if scheduled; (3) media: each agent moves toward the policy, if its ideology is positive, or toward its opposite, by $0.1\,|\text{ideology}|\,\tau_i$ of the distance; (4) 150 conversation attempts, each from a random agent to a random neighbour: half are blocked by the deployment cap, the rest pass the gate if the two opinions are within $\varepsilon$, and the listener then moves toward the speaker by $\mu = 0.15$ times the speaker's influence, which is halved after the enforcement lag if the speaker violates the policy (first dimension above the policy's threshold); (5) the policy pull: either the anchor, moving every agent $0.004$ of the way to the policy each tick, or the gated pull, $0.05$ of the way after each conversation that passes the gate; (6) from the enforcement lag on, every agent moves $0.04$ of the way toward its perceived policy, $\tau_i(p + b_i) + \eta$ shrunk toward its trust target.
 
-\section{Calibration and the gate--update crossing}
+\paragraph{Design concepts.} \emph{Stochasticity:} separate random streams for initialisation, shocks and perception noise, so that paired runs with and without shocks differ only in the shocks. \emph{Observation:} the variance of opinions (mean over dimensions) at the last tick.
+
+\paragraph{Initialisation.} Opinions and ideology uniform, trust Beta(2,\,2).
+
+\paragraph{Submodels.} \emph{Shocks:} at each tick, with probability 0.2, a shock of strength drawn from $[0.1,0.4]$ moves either the network's main hubs or ten random agents that fraction of the way toward the neutral centre. \emph{Perception:} the bias $b_i$ is the agent's ideology times a fixed per-dimension weight; the noise $\eta$ is Gaussian with standard deviation between 0.06 and 0.12 by dimension.
+
